\section PythonBindingsOverview Overview

The dependency-injection Python API is implemented by the pybind11 module
\c dakota.study._study.  Its C++ sources are in
\c dakota/src/python/study, its Python package is in
\c dakota/python/dakota/study, and its unit tests are in
\c dakota/src/unit.

Adding a class normally involves two distinct bindings:

<ol>
<li>Register the C++ type so Python can hold it, recognize its inheritance,
and call any intentionally exposed instance methods.</li>
<li>If Python users should construct the class, add a factory method to
\c Study::MethodFactory or \c Study::ModelFactory.  The factory accepts
already-constructed semantic dependencies plus a validated configuration
fragment.</li>
</ol>

Do not expose a \c ProblemDescDB constructor through this API.  New library
construction uses a component's \c IRStore constructor and a shared
\c StudyServices instance.  Child models, iterators, interfaces, variables,
and responses are passed as objects rather than recovered from pointer names.

The normal configuration flow is:

\verbatim
dict, keyword arguments, or Pydantic model
        |
        v
normalize_factory_config()       Python validation, exactly once
        |
        v
materialize_{method,model,...}()  validated JSON -> typed IRStore
        |
        v
Study factory / DI constructor    injected dependencies + shared services
\endverbatim

\section PythonBindingsPrerequisites C++ prerequisites

Before writing the Python binding, make sure the C++ library API is complete.
The class should have an IR-based constructor whose shape follows the nearby
component hierarchy.  For example, an iterator that consumes a model normally
accepts:

\code{.cpp}
NewIterator(const IRStore& method_store,
            std::shared_ptr<Model> model,
            std::shared_ptr<StudyServices> services);
\endcode

Some established hierarchies place \c StudyServices first.  Preserve the
class's C++ convention; the binding can adapt the ordering without exposing it
to Python.

The constructor must:

<ul>
<li>read its own configuration from \c IRStore;</li>
<li>accept semantic child objects directly;</li>
<li>reject null required children;</li>
<li>validate shared services with \c detail::validate_services(); and</li>
<li>preserve the existing \c ProblemDescDB construction path.</li>
</ul>

Validated IR contains every declared key, including defaults.  Read required
keys with \c store.get<T>("key") directly.  Do not guard reads with
\c contains(), supply ad hoc fallback values, or infer missing services from a
child.  A missing IR key is a contract error and should remain visible.

Add C++ construction coverage in
\c src/unit/dakota_di_construction/di_construction.cpp before or with the
Python binding.  Test successful construction, service identity, inconsistent
services, and null required dependencies.

\section PythonBindingsClass Register the class

Put study binding code in the file matching the component family:

<ul>
<li>\c BindIterator.cpp for iterators;</li>
<li>\c BindModel.cpp for models;</li>
<li>\c BindVariables.cpp, \c BindResponse.cpp, or \c BindInterface.cpp for
the corresponding value or interface type; and</li>
<li>\c BindStudyConfig.cpp for study-level configuration.</li>
</ul>

Register base classes before derived classes.  Components managed with
\c std::shared_ptr in C++ must use the same holder in pybind11:

\code{.cpp}
py::class_<NewIterator, Iterator, std::shared_ptr<NewIterator>>(
  m, "NewIterator", py::module_local());
\endcode

The base type must already be registered:

\code{.cpp}
py::class_<Iterator, std::shared_ptr<Iterator>>(
  m, "Iterator", py::module_local());
\endcode

Use \c py::module_local() consistently with the existing \c _study bindings.
Expose only useful public behavior.  Prefer a small lambda when a direct member
pointer is ambiguous, overloaded, or would expose an internal container:

\code{.cpp}
.def("num_responses",
     [](const NewIterator& iterator) {
       return iterator.all_responses().size();
     });
\endcode

Do not bind raw pointers or return references whose owner may be destroyed.
Use \c std::shared_ptr holders and pybind11 lifetime policies where ownership
is not already carried by the returned object.

\section PythonBindingsFactory Expose construction through Study

Most methods and models should be constructed through a \c Study factory so
all objects share the same \c StudyServices.  Add the C++ factory first in
\c Study.hpp and \c Study.cpp.  Its IR overload should perform construction;
the JSON overload should only validate/materialize and delegate.

For a method with one injected model, the Python signature should place the
semantic dependency first and configuration last:

\code{.py}
iterator = study.method.new_method(model, config)
iterator = study.method.new_method(model, samples=20)
\endcode

Bind the factory on \c Study::MethodFactory:

\code{.cpp}
factory.def("new_method",
  [](const Study::MethodFactory& self,
     std::shared_ptr<Model> model,
     const py::object& config, py::kwargs kwargs) {
    const auto fragment = normalize_factory_config(
      config, kwargs, "MethodFactory.new_method");
    const auto store = materialize_method(
      nlohmann::json{{"new_method", fragment}});
    return std::make_shared<NewIterator>(
      store, std::move(model), self.services());
  },
  py::arg("model").none(false), py::arg("config") = py::none());
\endcode

\c BindIterator.cpp provides \c bind_method() and \c construct<T>() helpers
for the common one-model iterator case.  Use those helpers when the class fits
their contract.  Write an explicit binding when dependencies or constructor
semantics differ.

For models, follow the explicit examples in \c BindStudy.cpp.  Keep required
dependencies non-null with \c py::arg("name").none(false).  Check elements of
injected vectors when pybind11 cannot enforce non-null entries itself.

Do not resolve child components from pointer strings in the configuration.
Pointer fields may still be materialized because the shared input grammar uses
them, but injected C++ objects are the semantic dependencies in library mode.

\section PythonBindingsConfig Validate and materialize configuration

The Python API accepts one of three exclusive configuration forms:

\code{.py}
factory(model, config={"samples": 20})
factory(model, config=SamplingConfig(samples=20))
factory(model, samples=20)
\endcode

Do not accept both \c config and configuration keyword arguments.  Route all
three forms through \c normalize_factory_config() in
\c python/dakota/study/_validation.py.  This function validates dictionaries,
trusts an instance of the expected Pydantic class, serializes to JSON form, and
prevents accidental revalidation.

Add the factory-to-schema mapping to \c _FACTORY_CONFIG_CLASSES:

\code{.py}
"MethodFactory.new_method": method.NewMethodConfig,
\endcode

If the class is selected by the generated method grammar, confirm that the
generic registration loop already discovers it before adding a manual entry.
The schema models and their materialization metadata are authoritative; do not
reimplement schema defaults or selector rules in the binding.  See
\ref PydanticSpec for grammar and materialization changes.

After normalization, wrap the fragment with its selection keyword before
materialization:

\code{.cpp}
materialize_method(nlohmann::json{{"new_method", fragment}})
\endcode

Use \c materialize_model(), \c materialize_variables(),
\c materialize_interface(), or \c materialize_responses() for other block
types.  Materialization happens after Python validation; it is not a second
user-input validation layer.

Literal selectors use Boolean \c true, whereas configurable selectors use an
object.  For example, a field declared as \c Literal[True] is represented by
\c {"quadratic": true}, not \c {"quadratic": {}}.  Consult the actual
Pydantic annotation rather than copying a superficially similar selector.

\section PythonBindingsModule Register and package the binding

Each binding file exposes one registration function declared in
\c DakotaStudyPython.hpp and called from the \c PYBIND11_MODULE block in
\c DakotaStudyPython.cpp.  If adding a new binding file or registration group:

<ol>
<li>Declare \c bind_new_classes(py::module_& m) in
\c DakotaStudyPython.hpp.</li>
<li>Implement it in \c src/python/study/BindNewClasses.cpp.</li>
<li>Add that source file to \c dakota_study_python_src in
\c src/CMakeLists.txt.</li>
<li>Call \c bind_new_classes(m) from \c PYBIND11_MODULE(_study, m), after any
base classes it depends on have been registered.</li>
</ol>

For a class added to an existing binding group, only the existing group
function normally changes.

Export public names from \c python/dakota/study/__init__.py.  Unconditional
classes can appear in the main import list.  Classes controlled by optional
libraries must be imported conditionally or exported through the existing
iterator manifest so importing \c dakota.study still works when the feature is
disabled.

The \c _study target is placed directly in the build-tree
\c dakota/study package and installed by \c src/CMakeLists.txt.  Do not add a
second extension module solely for one class.

\section PythonBindingsOptional Optional dependencies

Match the feature guard used by the C++ implementation in all relevant places:

<ul>
<li>the class header include;</li>
<li>the \c py::class_ registration;</li>
<li>the factory binding;</li>
<li>the public Python export; and</li>
<li>tests that assert availability.</li>
</ul>

For example, a class requiring DOT must remain inside \c HAVE_DOT guards.
Tests should skip or test absence when the dependency is unavailable; they
must not make importing the package fail.

\section PythonBindingsTests Add unit tests

Place Python unit tests in \c src/unit and use the standard-library
\c unittest framework.  Register them in \c src/unit/CMakeLists.txt with the
\c Unit and \c Python labels and the build-tree \c PYTHONPATH.  Do not place
Dakota unit tests under \c python/tests.

At minimum, test:

<ul>
<li>the class is exported and has the expected base type;</li>
<li>dict, Pydantic-model, keyword, and supported positional configuration
forms construct the same component type;</li>
<li>configuration inputs are not mutated;</li>
<li>supplying both \c config and keyword configuration fails;</li>
<li>unknown fields and wrong config types fail during validation;</li>
<li>required semantic dependencies reject \c None and wrong Python types;</li>
<li>optional dependencies behave correctly when omitted; and</li>
<li>the constructed object can execute a small meaningful operation when the
required driver is available.</li>
</ul>

When calling \c model_validate() inside a test, pass a deep copy if generated
before-validators may transform nested dictionaries.  Otherwise the test can
mutate its own fixture before the factory is called and incorrectly attribute
that mutation to production code.

Run the focused tests through CTest so they use the same extension module and
environment as CI.  Typical commands are:

\verbatim
ctest --test-dir build -R dakota_python_study --output-on-failure
ctest --test-dir build -R dakota_di_construction --output-on-failure
\endverbatim

Also run the relevant configuration-normalization tests directly with
\c python -m unittest when the build-tree package is available.

\section PythonBindingsChecklist Review checklist

Before submitting a new binding, verify:

<ul>
<li>The C++ class has a tested IR/DI constructor.</li>
<li>The binding uses the correct base class and \c std::shared_ptr holder.</li>
<li>Base classes are registered before derived classes.</li>
<li>Construction goes through the owning \c Study factory and shared services.</li>
<li>Semantic dependencies are injected as objects, not reconstructed from
pointer names.</li>
<li>Configuration is validated once, then materialized to the correct block
type.</li>
<li>Required IR keys are read directly without \c contains() fallbacks.</li>
<li>Python argument order is dependencies first and configuration last.</li>
<li>Optional build guards are consistent across C++, Python exports, and tests.</li>
<li>Tests live in \c src/unit, use \c unittest, and are registered with CTest.</li>
<li>Both Python and C++ DI construction tests pass.</li>
</ul>

*/

} // namespace Dakota
